UNIT capitalize
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG capitalize(string: any, opts?: boolean | object | any) -> string
#   This is the package's MAIN / default export (a single function, exportName=null, importKind=default).
#   In the PUBLISHED CommonJS package the function is exposed as `module.exports = capitalize`.
#   >>> EMISSION EXPORT-SHAPE (READ THIS — it is a CONTRACT, not a behavioral note) <<<
#   The clean-room emission file is an ESM `.mjs` module and the grader LOADS it with
#   `await import(pathToFileURL(path).href)` then resolves `m.default`. Therefore the re-emitter
#   MUST expose the function as the ESM DEFAULT export:  `export default capitalize;`
#   DO NOT write `module.exports = capitalize` (CommonJS) — in a `.mjs` file `module` is undefined
#   and there is no `default` export, so the grader's `loadFn` returns undefined and EVERY emission
#   fails to load (0-quorum). See section EXPORT-SHAPE (EMISSION) below. This export-format choice
#   does NOT change any behavior; it only changes the module wrapper the function is published under.

DEPENDS-ON none
# Zero runtime dependencies. Pure value transform. No clock/RNG/env/fs/net seam.
# The package ALSO exposes capitalize.words (a separate function). The MAIN export's
# output does NOT depend on capitalize.words in any way (it never calls it). capitalize.words
# is OUT-OF-CONTRACT for this unit and is NOT exercised by the oracle.

# ============================================================================================
# EXPORT-SHAPE (EMISSION)  —  HARD CONTRACT for the BLIND re-emitter
# ============================================================================================
# The unit is re-emitted into an ESM module file with extension `.mjs` (runs/emit_<i>.mjs).
# The grader imports that file as ECMAScript Module and reads the DEFAULT export:
#       const m = await import(pathToFileURL(path).href);
#       const fn = m.default ?? <first function-valued named export>;
# So the emitted module MUST make the `capitalize` function its ESM DEFAULT export. Emit, verbatim
# in spirit:
#
#       function capitalize(string, opts) { ... exact body per BEHAVIOR ... }
#       function normalizeOptions(opts)   { ... exact body per BEHAVIOR ... }
#       export default capitalize;
#
#   - REQUIRED: `export default capitalize;`  (an ESM default export of the function).
#     Equivalent acceptable forms: `export default function capitalize(string, opts) { ... }`.
#   - FORBIDDEN in the `.mjs` emission: `module.exports = capitalize;`, `exports.default = ...`,
#     `module.exports.default = ...`, or anything relying on the CommonJS `module`/`exports`
#     globals. Those are undefined in an ESM `.mjs` file and yield NO `default` export, so the
#     grader cannot load the function (this is exactly what produced the 0/3 quorum in round 1 —
#     a LOAD failure, not a behavioral divergence).
#   - A function-valued NAMED export (e.g. `export function capitalize(...) {}` with no default)
#     would also be tolerated by the grader's fallback, but the DEFAULT export is the canonical
#     and unambiguous target — prefer `export default capitalize;`.
#   - exportName=null / importKind=default in the input-generator reflects "call the default
#     export directly"; it is consistent with `export default` in the emission.
# This section governs ONLY the module wrapper / export statement. The function BODY and all
# observable behavior are specified by BEHAVIOR below and are UNCHANGED.
# (The published upstream package is CommonJS `module.exports = capitalize`; that is the real
#  package's default-export semantics and is documented in PROVENANCE. The `.mjs` clean-room
#  emission re-expresses that SAME single-default-export shape in ESM as `export default`.)
# ============================================================================================

BEHAVIOR
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The unit is exactly the 3-line body of the main export plus the normalizeOptions helper.
# Pseudocode of the EXACT pipeline (reproduce precisely):
#
#   capitalize(string, opts):
#     opts = normalizeOptions(opts)
#     if (!opts.preserve):               # JS truthiness of opts.preserve
#       string = string.toLowerCase()    # default (locale-independent) Unicode lowercasing
#     return string.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + string.substring(1)
#                                          # charAt(0)/substring operate on UTF-16 CODE UNITS
#
#   normalizeOptions(opts):
#     if (!opts) return { preserve: false }          # opts falsy -> see NODE-OPT-A
#     if (typeof opts === 'boolean') return { preserve: opts }   # only `true` reaches here -> NODE-OPT-B
#     if (opts.skipWord instanceof RegExp) { ...wrap opts.skipWord... }  # NODE-OPT-C (no effect on main output)
#     return opts                                    # any other truthy value returned as-is -> NODE-OPT-D
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# NODE-1  Option normalization (resolve the effective `preserve` flag). Determined by the
#         RUNTIME TYPE / TRUTHINESS of the SECOND arg `opts`:
#
#   NODE-OPT-A  opts is FALSY  (i.e. opts is one of: undefined / absent / null / false / 0 / "" / NaN):
#               effective preserve = false  (normalizeOptions returns { preserve:false }).
#
#   NODE-OPT-B  opts === true (boolean true; boolean false already handled by OPT-A as falsy):
#               effective preserve = true   (normalizeOptions returns { preserve:true }).
#
#   NODE-OPT-C  opts is a truthy object whose `skipWord` property is a RegExp:
#               normalizeOptions MUTATES opts.skipWord into a wrapper function, then returns opts.
#               This mutation is a side effect on the caller's object but DOES NOT change the main
#               export's RETURN VALUE (the main export never reads opts.skipWord). For the oracle's
#               value contract, treat this exactly like OPT-D: effective preserve = truthiness of
#               opts.preserve (undefined if absent -> false).
#
#   NODE-OPT-D  opts is any OTHER truthy value (plain object, string, number != 0, array, etc.):
#               normalizeOptions returns opts UNCHANGED. effective preserve = Boolean(opts.preserve).
#               - For a plain object: read the `preserve` property and take its truthiness.
#                   { preserve:true } -> true ; { preserve:1 } -> true ; { preserve:"x" } -> true
#                   { preserve:false }-> false; { preserve:0 } -> false; { preserve:null }-> false
#                   {} or { foo:1 }   -> preserve undefined -> false
#               - For a truthy string / number / array (no own meaningful `preserve`): reading
#                   `.preserve` yields undefined -> false. So opts="x", opts=1, opts=[] => preserve=false.
#
# NODE-2  Lowercasing of the source string (the `!opts.preserve` branch):
#         IF effective preserve is FALSY -> string := string.toLowerCase().
#         IF effective preserve is TRUTHY -> string is left UNCHANGED (the rest of its casing is
#         preserved). Uses String.prototype.toLowerCase (the DEFAULT, non-locale variant). Full
#         Unicode default case mapping over UTF-16; e.g. "İstanbul".toLowerCase() etc. are the
#         engine defaults (NOT toLocaleLowerCase). No locale is consulted.
#
# NODE-3  Capitalize first code unit and concatenate the remainder:
#         return  string.charAt(0).toUpperCase()  +  string.substring(1)
#         - charAt(0): the FIRST UTF-16 CODE UNIT of the (possibly-lowercased) string. For the
#           empty string charAt(0) === "" and substring(1) === "" -> result "".
#         - .toUpperCase() on that single code unit uses String.prototype.toUpperCase (DEFAULT,
#           non-locale). It can EXPAND to multiple chars: e.g. "ß" -> "SS", the "fi" ligature
#           U+FB01 -> "FI". A non-cased first code unit (digit, space, punctuation, a LONE SURROGATE
#           from an astral/emoji char) is returned unchanged by toUpperCase.
#         - .substring(1): the rest of the string from code-unit index 1 to the end, UNCHANGED.
#
# DETERMINISTIC EDGE CASES (all VERIFIED by running capitalize@2.0.4):
#   ""                              -> ""                       # empty
#   "a"                             -> "A"
#   "A"                             -> "A"
#   "Hello"                         -> "Hello"
#   "united states"                 -> "United states"          # only FIRST char capitalized; rest lowercased
#   "UNITED STATES"                 -> "United states"           # rest lowercased by default
#   "uNiTeD sTaTeS"                 -> "United states"
#   "1abc"                          -> "1abc"                    # leading digit: toUpperCase no-op
#   " hi"                           -> " hi"                     # leading space: no-op; rest already lower
#   "!hi"                           -> "!hi"
#   "a b"                           -> "A b"                     # internal whitespace preserved
#   "ab "                           -> "Ab "                     # trailing whitespace preserved
#   "   "                           -> "   "                     # all whitespace
#   "\tab"                          -> "\tab"                    # leading tab: no-op
#   "12345"                         -> "12345"
#   "caña"                          -> "Caña"                    # Latin accented (default casing)
#   "ñam"                           -> "Ñam"
#   "über"                          -> "Über"
#   "привет"                        -> "Привет"                  # Cyrillic
#   "ßomething"                     -> "SSomething"              # ß first -> "SS" (expansion)
#   "ﬁle"  (ﬁle)               -> "FIle"                    # ligature first -> "FI"
#   "istanbul"                      -> "Istanbul"                # default (non-Turkish) casing
#   "😀abc"  (U+1F600 + "abc")      -> "😀abc"                   # first CODE UNIT is a lone high surrogate;
#                                                                #  toUpperCase no-op, result unchanged
#   "😀"                            -> "😀"                      # single astral char unchanged
#   "𝕏yz"   (U+1D54F + "yz")        -> "𝕏yz"                     # astral first code unit unchanged
#
#   OPTION CASES (string fixed where shown):
#   ("canDoItRight", true)          -> "CanDoItRight"            # preserve rest's casing
#   ("canDoItRight", false)         -> "Candoitright"            # false is falsy -> lowercase
#   ("HELLO", true)                 -> "HELLO"
#   ("HELLO", false)                -> "Hello"
#   ("HELLO", {preserve:true})      -> "HELLO"
#   ("HELLO", {preserve:false})     -> "Hello"
#   ("HELLO", {})                   -> "Hello"
#   ("HELLO", {foo:1})              -> "Hello"
#   ("HELLO", {preserve:1})         -> "HELLO"                   # truthy preserve
#   ("HELLO", {preserve:0})         -> "Hello"
#   ("HELLO", {preserve:null})      -> "Hello"
#   ("HELLO", {preserve:"x"})       -> "HELLO"                   # non-empty string is truthy
#   ("HELLO", "x")                  -> "Hello"                   # truthy non-bool opts; .preserve undefined
#   ("HELLO", 1)                    -> "Hello"
#   ("HELLO", 0)                    -> "Hello"                   # 0 falsy -> {preserve:false}
#   ("HELLO", "")                   -> "Hello"                   # "" falsy
#   ("HELLO", [])                   -> "Hello"                   # array truthy; .preserve undefined
#   ("HELLO", null)                 -> "Hello"
#   ("HELLO", {skipWord:/x/})       -> "Hello"                   # skipWord irrelevant to main export; preserve undefined
#   ("hello", {skipWord:/x/, preserve:true}) -> "Hello"          # preserve true; "ello" already lower
#   ("", true)                      -> ""
#   ("", {preserve:true})           -> ""
#
# NODE-THROW  NON-STRING `string` argument (the unit performs NO coercion — String() is never called):
#   The body immediately does string.toLowerCase() (or, on the preserve path, string.charAt(0)),
#   so any non-string `string` throws a TypeError at runtime. EXACT messages (V8 / Node) by type:
#     - number / boolean / object / array / Symbol-less primitive box  ->
#         TypeError: "string.toLowerCase is not a function"
#         (or on the preserve:true path: "string.charAt is not a function")
#     - null      -> TypeError: "Cannot read properties of null (reading 'toLowerCase')"
#                    (preserve path: "... (reading 'charAt')")
#     - undefined / no-arg -> TypeError: "Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'toLowerCase')"
#                    (preserve path: "... (reading 'charAt')")
#   The frozen oracle records a throw as `{__throw}` (the BLIND re-emitter must also throw a
#   TypeError here). The generator DOES emit codec-faithful non-string `string` values that are
#   GRADABLE as throw-paths (number, boolean, null, plain object, array, bigint). It does NOT
#   emit `undefined` as the `string` arg because `undefined` is NOT codec-round-trippable as an
#   argument element; the undefined/no-arg throw is documented here as OUT-OF-ENVELOPE for the
#   generator but fully specified for a blind re-emitter.

ORACLE value -> oracle.json

SCOPE
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
#   (a) EXPORT SHAPE (emission): the function is published as the ESM DEFAULT export of the `.mjs`
#       file — `export default capitalize;` (see EXPORT-SHAPE (EMISSION) above). This is in-contract
#       because the grader loads `m.default`; a CommonJS `module.exports` in a `.mjs` file is a LOAD
#       failure. It does not alter any computed value.
#   (b) The full main-export pipeline above — normalizeOptions (the falsy / boolean-true /
#       regex-skipWord / pass-through branches), the `!opts.preserve` lowercasing branch, and
#       `charAt(0).toUpperCase() + substring(1)` over UTF-16 code units using the DEFAULT
#       (non-locale) toUpperCase/toLowerCase. The throw-on-non-string behavior with the exact
#       TypeError class. Full Unicode default case mapping (accents, Cyrillic, ß-expansion,
#       ligature-expansion, lone-surrogate no-op) is delegated to the runtime's String case
#       methods and is in-scope as "whatever the engine's default toUpperCase/toLowerCase produce"
#       — the blind re-emitter reproduces it by calling the same default String case methods (NOT a
#       locale variant, NOT a hand-rolled table).
#
# CARRIED DATA: none. There is no large literal table; the only literals (QUOTE, WORD, nonWord
#   regexes) belong to capitalize.words, which is out-of-contract. No carried.json is shipped.
#
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT:
#   - capitalize.words (the secondary export) — separate function; not exercised by this unit's oracle.
#   - The MUTATION side effect of normalizeOptions on a caller-supplied opts object whose
#     `skipWord` is a RegExp (it rewrites opts.skipWord into a function). This is a side effect on
#     the input object, never observable in the main export's RETURN VALUE; the value-mode oracle
#     does not grade it. The generator avoids RegExp-valued skipWord to keep inputs codec-clean.
#
# NON-ROUND-TRIPPABLE / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented, NOT emitted by genInputs):
#   - `string` = undefined (or no-arg call): throws TypeError ("... reading 'toLowerCase'"); undefined
#     is not a codec-faithful argument element, so it is specified here but not generated.
#   - opts containing a live function (skipWord callback) or a RegExp: not codec-round-trippable in a
#     way that affects the main export's value (and RegExp is irrelevant to the return); not generated.

PROVENANCE capitalize@2.0.4 — https://github.com/grncdr/js-capitalize — MIT —
  source file: node_modules/capitalize/index.js (function `capitalize` + helper `normalizeOptions`).
  In the PUBLISHED CommonJS package the main/default export is `module.exports = capitalize` (a single
  function; this is the package's real default-export semantics). The `.mjs` clean-room EMISSION
  re-expresses that same single-default-export shape in ESM as `export default capitalize;` so the ESM
  grader (`await import(...)` -> `m.default`) can load it — see EXPORT-SHAPE (EMISSION). Behavior
  verified by executing capitalize@2.0.4 under Node.
